reason={
'opr-marriage-01':'The 1681 Carstairs bride is a separate identity question from Agnes with Swan in 1666, Paul in 1670 and Anderson at Longformacus. Inveresk siblings Agnes (1634), Thomas (1637) and Marion (1642) have the indexed father Thomas, but no inspected Carstairs clause joins either Agnes or the 1679 Thomas bridegroom to that family. Thomson-263 has an Edinburgh Peirie household; its attached children add no Carstairs bridge and no dated competing household at 1681. Retain it as a weaker compatible spouse lead.',
'opr-marriage-04':'The printed marriage register adds John Bruce’s trade of smith, independently of the generic OPR index. Glasgow-3143 is already supported as Marion who married the Edinburgh weaver William Calder in June 1686; that established life segment is not transferred into this 1684 event. Bruce-6113 represents Sir John Bruce of Kinross with the 1687 Christian Leslie marriage, whereas Bruce-3507 has only a speculative Clackmannan son whose own profile explicitly leaves his baptism/parentage open. Neither inspected family supplies this smith-and-Marion couple. Different spouses alone are not exclusions; keep both candidate husbands qualified and the events separate.',
'opr-marriage-06':'One documentary marriage/banns account contains both unnamed groom and Margaret. The Glasgow surname is explicitly assumed, the groom has no given name and Margaret has no surname. Consolidate the three queue aliases into one event account. There is no recorded child or named parent to attach; the 1679 Carstairs Thomas event shares the date but has a different parish, parties and reference and remains separate.',
'opr-marriage-08':'The printed Edinburgh register identifies Alexander as a tailor and dates his marriage to Eupham to 8 December 1671. The same-name tailor’s Edinburgh burgess admission on 22 June 1670 records apprenticeship to the late Alexander Thomsone (31 January 1655); this is a strong occupational lead for the groom, not a father clause. Thomsone is a master, not a parent. Eupham Glasgow-3358’s 1651 Edinburgh child index names Robert Glasgow/Eupham Lorimer; Glasgow-1167’s 1634 event has a documented Glasgow/Glassford father conflict. Neither child-to-bride identification is proved. The Channelkirk September event is provisionally one-couple related but stays a distinct register occurrence.',
'opr-marriage-10':'Woodside-784 is supported as father Robert of James Woodside with Jonet White, baptised 30 January 1676, through the child profile. This is an earlier Ayrshire household, not a concrete exclusion of a 1700 later marriage to Jean. Jean candidates’ individual baptism families remain distinct. No current parent/child or spouse record read connects this couple to the West Kilbride 13 January 1700 event.',
'opr-marriage-11':'The inspected printed page calls William Mitchell a merchant in his marriage to Jean on 16 December 1675. Mitchell-54981’s attached daughter Mary Mitchell-54795 explicitly questions her own paternal assignment; the Carolina birth placement is estimated and its 1720 deed does not establish this William’s identity. It is not used to exclude an earlier/later marriage. Glasgow-1163’s 1650 Edinburgh child index names Thomas Glasgow/Catharine Weir; it is a compatible local child candidate, not proof that those are the bride’s parents.',
'opr-marriage-13':'The 1670 Agnes–Patrik Paul event remains separate from the 1666 Agnes–James Swan event, even in the same parish. The supplied births index identifies local Agnes in 1677 to Alexander, which is a different child-record assertion and does not name the 1670 bride’s father. Unrestricted Patrick/Patrik Paul searches from the first audit found no surviving historical match; no new bridge appeared in the cross-record review.',
'opr-marriage-14':'The printed page identifies George Cargill as bookseller and Alison as his bride on 11 March 1651. A nearby George Cargill, printer, marries Elspeth Orme in 1663; it is a second documentary occurrence and is not asserted to be the same husband. Newly completed Allison/Alesoun/Alisoun Glasgow and Alisone/Alice Glesgow API variants returned no historical Open candidate. Neither bookseller’s parentage nor Alison’s father is given here.',
'opr-marriage-18':'Walston 1690 Margaret–John Tailzer is separate from the Cambusnethan 1693 Margaret–John Aiton event. The 1643 Glasgow baptism names William Glasgow/Bessie Howie and belongs to Glasgow-1524; the same date imported on Glasgow-3050 does not prove Thomas Glasgow/Agnes Thomsone parentage. Keep these as two profile identities. Taylor-32027 has an unsourced Fife placement and no wife/child bridge to this event.',
'opr-marriage-25':'The 1652 St Monance Jeane–Robert Aliesone occurrence predates Glasgow-1163’s documented 1650 Edinburgh childhood and does not fit that represented child as a bride. Existing exclusions remain. New unrestricted Robert Aliesoun/Allisone API variants found no historical Open profile; this is a profile search, not a search of the parish’s full baptism collection. No record read names either spouse’s father.',
'opr-marriage-26':'Longformacus 1666 Agnas–William Anderson is not combined into a remarriage biography with the Kirknewton Swan/Paul events. Anderson-3493’s cited 1632 Alloa baptism names William Andersone/Margarat Crokit, proving that child’s record parentage, not that of the Longformacus groom. Agnes candidates’ indexed natal households are compared individually; no Longformacus family clause bridges them.',
'opr-marriage-27':'The Kirknewton 12 May 1666 Agnes–James Swan occurrence is not merged with the 8 July 1670 Agnes–Patrik Paul occurrence. Swan-736’s represented family is with Alisone Peibles at Elie, married 1631; the wife’s estimated 1663 death permits a later marriage, so the different earlier spouse is not an exclusion. Its child William Swan has no inspected connection to this Agnes or Kirknewton.',
'opr-marriage-32':'Channelkirk 17 September 1671 Effie–Alexander Hutchesone is kept as a distinct event, provisionally associated with the Edinburgh 8 December 1671 Eupham–Alexander tailor event. Same couple is plausible under Effie/Eupham and Hutcheson variants, but the difference of parish and date does not establish banns followed by marriage or identity with a named father. The occupational fact read at Edinburgh is not assigned as a quoted Channelkirk fact.',
'opr-marriage-38':'Cambusnethan 1693 Margrat–John Aiton is separate from Walston 1690 Margaret–John Tailzer. Ayton-398 represents the Fawside/West Herrington Durham estate family and has an unnamed wife and a 1702 will; child Samuel Ayton-399 supplies no wife-name bridge. Distant residence alone does not exclude it. The direct 1643 Glasgow child index supports William/Bessie parentage only for that child record, not the Cambusnethan bride.',
'opr-marriage-39':'The printed register preserves surname Rait (Raite/Rate), John’s trade as tailor and the 28 June 1659 marriage to Marion. Reid-24609 represents the Edinburgh Reid–Helen Henderson family, with son George Reade-97 in Maryland; the surname is a weaker phonetic lead and no tailor/Marion bridge is established. The unrestricted Raitt search and full biographies identify Raitt-2 as the 1806 Captain and Raitt-360 as the 1866-born Western Australian butcher, excluding both concretely. Other printed Rait entries do not identify this husband’s father.',
'opr-marriage-41':'The Carstairs 1679 Thomas–Gyles Spence entry is separate from the Strathblane unnamed event on the same day. Thomas Glasgow-1449 is newly excluded by his represented later family: father Glasgow-1382 is explicitly born about 1766 and dies 1851; mother Christine Davidson has documented motherhood of Nathaniel in 1774, incompatible with being the mother of a 1679 adult. Glasgow-1226 remains a weaker possibility because fatherhood through 1735 would imply an older father but is not impossible. Graduate Glasgow-3413’s 1670 degree does not establish his assumed Thomas Glasgow-3187 father, and the 1660 burgess entry gives no father. Newly searched Gellies/Gilles/Giles Spence/Spens/Spense yielded no historical woman; the Tudor male Spencer lead remains excluded.',
'opr-marriage-44':'The printed register identifies John Williamson as a cordiner in the 14 November 1672 Alison marriage. Father John Williamson-14320’s inspected child profiles preserve Kirkcaldy index entries with Christian Turpie in 1674, 1677 and 1680, plus their 1655 marriage and her 1690 death entry. This continuing named-couple sequence conflicts with identifying him as the Edinburgh Alison bridegroom and is a substantive competing-household exclusion, not merely another spouse’s name. Son Williamson-14321, baptised 1657, is still a compatible but young candidate with no child or occupation bridge. Alison remains without a compatible profile; the row’s spouse candidates must not change her own no-profile status.',
'opr-marriage-47':'The 1684 Glasgow Isob–James Coldwell occurrence has candidate women with different indexed natal families. Caldwell-12289 has a before-1670 bound but cites children in Scotland and Maryland in 1691/1692, rather than a secure own birth. Those later records permit a 1684 marriage and do not identify this Isob. No newly read record bridges an Isob baptism father to this bride.',
'opr-marriage-51':'The 1661 Glasgow Joannet–Andrew Allexander record is compared to the Glasgow 1622 Jonet child of John/Jonet Mathie and 1636 Jonet child of William/Bessie Howie. Their father links are already marked confident (20) and are not new upgrades. Andrew Alexander-7545’s Ulster estate-family segment is an alternative candidate, not evidence for a Glasgow father. The newly captured children are Ulster-family profiles; they provide no Joannet connection. Late upper-bound Janet candidates remain lower-ranked unless an actual incompatible family event is established.',
'opr-marriage-54':'The Carriden 30 September 1686 Marion–John Gray occurrence remains separate from the Edinburgh 29 June 1686 Marion–William Calder marriage. Glasgow-3143 is already supported as the Calder wife by her 1657 baptism and the Calder burgess admission naming James Glasgow, weaver, as father. A later September marriage would require a further event, but no husband death is established, so this alone is not a concrete exclusion. Gray-27691 and Gray-32439 have Fife family placements without a Marion/Carriden bridge; retain qualified candidates.',
'opr-754-10-113':'The 1675 Polwarth index other-details TOD / WILLIAM GLASGOW leaves William’s role unknown. Todd-7464’s children have estimated birth years, including circa 1680; the inspected child biographies do not establish dated births after Janet’s 1675 burial, so those guesses do not exclude her. Glasgow-1104 and Glasgow-1522 remain possible under a natal-Glasgow reading; their established father links are already confident and William in this death entry is not reassigned as father. No record read links her to the 1656/1658 Polwarth children.',
'opr-705-10-183':'The Dirleton 1688 index supplies no forename, age or kinship. It is one burial/death occurrence, not evidence for an unnamed child or a separate parent. No distinct-person name search can be made from this entry. It is kept separate from the 1679 Strathblane event. The original page remains required to discover any identifying wording.'}
